Extension API: one ship can be selected ON THE FLEET SCREEN.

STATUS: applied locally to ~/orion2re (branch orionlayer-local, commit
e6199966) by work order 134 C, 19 September 2026. Required by
tools/version_check.py. NOT CONFIRMED LIVE — work order 134's live part
is parked (doc/briefs/134-parked-for-data.md); it compiles and the
client sends it, and that is all that has been shown. Apply
doc/ext_fleet_screen_state.patch first: this is its write half and the
client needs both. It comes back off with

    cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
        ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_fleet_screen_select.patch

then rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    On the fleet screen a single ship's selection cannot be changed from
    outside at all, and the three doors are each shut for a different
    reason:

      - A CLICK. The per-ship toggle is painted in the DRAW pass, which
        tests mouse::Mouse_Button_() for the LIVE button state
        (flt1.cpp:413-436). An injected click is a buffered event, and by
        the time the draw pass looks the button has gone up again. This
        is open fix 20's finding on the fleet box, at a second site.
      - ACTIVATE_FIELD on a big icon. It reaches
        Scan_Fltscrn_Big_Icons_ result 0, which only SCANS the ship —
        the stats panel follows the pointer, the selection does not
        (flt2.cpp:924-928, flt1.cpp:615-620).
      - MSG_SELECT_SHIP, i.e. open fix 21. It refuses unless
        MOVEBOX::Moveable_Box_Selected_(2), and the fleet screen closes
        box 2; and it writes _ship_node[].selected, which this screen
        never reads — it paints from _fltscrn_big_icon[].selected
        (flt1.cpp:429). Two independent reasons, so widening one of them
        would not have been enough.

    What is left is ALL (flt1.cpp:684-687), which selects or clears the
    whole list. So without this, a SUBSET of a stack cannot be moved or
    scrapped from outside — the two things the screen exists for.

WHAT IT DOES

    MSG_SELECT_SHIP (0x85, open fix 21) branches on the screen:
    SCREEN_FLEET goes to a new static Select_Fltscrn_Ship_, everything
    else keeps Select_Ship_ unchanged. ONE message id because it is one
    question — "select this ship" — and two handlers because the two
    screens keep the answer in different arrays. The branch is on the
    screen and not on which handler happens to return 1, so a refusal on
    one screen can never be mistaken for an attempt on the other.

    Select_Fltscrn_Ship_ makes the original's own checks, in its order:

        MOX::_current_screen == SCREEN_FLEET
        MOX::_PLAYER_NUM == MOX::_fltscrn_stack_owner   flt1.cpp:1185, :1193
        FLT1::_relocate_button_mode != 1                flt1.cpp:413
        _fltscrn_big_icon != nullptr
        the ship is IN the displayed list               flt2.cpp:207-268

    and then writes the one line ALL writes:

        _fltscrn_big_icon[i].selected = selected ? 1 : 0

    (Set_Fltscrn_Big_Icons_, flt1.cpp:1610-1624). Returns 1 applied,
    0 refused.

    NOTHING ELSE IS MAINTAINED HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The loop's
    own Update_Selection_Flags_ (flt1.cpp:811, :1069-1093) recounts
    _n_big_icons_selected and mirrors _fltscrn_icon_selection_status[]
    on the next iteration — which is exactly what happens after ALL, so
    the command leaves the engine in a state it already reaches by
    itself, and the SCRAP button appears or goes on the same rebuild
    (flt1.cpp:1185).

COST IF IT IS NOT APPLIED

    Nothing changes for the game. For a client, the HD fleet screen can
    show the selection (open fix 27 carries it) but cannot change it
    except through ALL, and says so.

